n to see  this for the crime that it really is  nothing much is going to change so how  do you go about doing that and what  would your message be for example for  the men who are watching this interview  right now  well first of all I would say it is a  human rights violation so violence  against women any kind of violence is  actually not just abuse it is a human  rights violation so if you are indulging  in that kind of behavior you are  violating that woman's human right the  second thing that I think is really  important is that we have to keep  advocating for the importance of  tackling this issue as a public policy  priority the problem is that for most  governments this isn't a big enough  priority yes on days like the  international day to end violence  against women you will have public  figures speak up about it today they'll  speak up about it over the next 16 days  because we have a campaign for 16 days  but it is actually got to be something  that's discussed in government in  cabinet and it has got to rise to the  level of a national priority so that  there are resources allocated to it and  it is something that is viewed as a  universal problem and not just an  individual house sort problem that has  to be faced by the woman who's the  victim of abuse and the U.N has  recognized that the overall number of  femicides has remained largely unchanged  over the past decade what is going wrong  there  well there are a couple of things  happening first as I said Society has  really normalized violence against women  people are no longer a shot enough about  it to want to take action the second  thing that's happened is that during the  pandemic there was a huge exponential  rise in violence against women and it  became visible during the pandemic our  Secretary General called it the shadow  pandemic but a post-pandemic those raids  of violence continue to remain high and  the third thing that has happened in the  last couple of years is that the number  of conflicts in the world and the number  of countries in the world where there is  a huge pushback against women's rights  has gone up so whether it's Afghanistan  or Iran or what's happening but the  conflict in Ukraine you see women  suffering because they are in the front  lines of either conflict or protesting  against oppression of their  and so that is associated with the right  right a rise in the rates of violence  so these are intertwined issues  requiring  urgent responses Anita Bhatia I want to  thank you so much for joining us on DW  news to speak about these questions  thank you
 

